# An Explanation of “God”
Tamil title: கடவுள் விளக்கம்

Today we see superstition disappearing and rational thought developing among most people. As a result, people desire answers to the many doubts whose resolution will assist rational development. Foremost among those doubts is the demand for a clear explanation of what the word *kadavul*, “God,” means.

Many wish for an extensive explanation. Yet the growing rational world does not greatly desire lengthy treatment of questions of this kind. Taking account also of present conditions and the shortage of paper, I have fulfilled my intention to give a concise explanation through *Dravida Nadu*.

I do not wish to introduce religious textual citations that alternately reject and accept claims, present mutually opposed ideas and internally contradictory doctrines, and leave both students and listeners bewildered between “that” and “this.” I therefore do not seek textual authority.

Trying to remove doubts through texts merely produces doubt again and again. When explaining something according to the age, the people's intellectual growth and natural qualities, citing mutually contradictory books only supports further ignorance.

This essay is written with an investigative spirit compatible with reason. Its readers must therefore approach it without deceit, without partisan hatred or delight, without saying one thing after seeing another, and without irritation.

Every word in our precious Tamil language resembles a fine pomegranate. Break open that fruit and jewel-like seeds shine, ripened with delicious juice. Likewise, enter the Tamil treasury, take a classical Tamil word and open it calmly: countless gems of knowledge buried within flash one after another like lightning.

Everyone knows that the Tamil word *velai* means work. The same word also denotes the sea. It suits the sea, the writer says, because the sea ceaselessly performs the work of beating waves. Thousands of such words reveal the undiminished excellence of the classical Tamil treasury.

Some people learn only that Tamil contains thousands of words explaining several meanings, then assign them inappropriate meanings and begin murdering Tamil. Alas, what excellent intelligence! Religious leaders stand foremost among these murderers of Tamil, and their destruction has no limit.

The distinctively Tamil word *kadavul*, now employed by religious writers, does not in the least fit the meaning they give it. Its true significance will become clear through ideas explaining the word.

*Kadavul* is a Tamil word referring, according to this essay, only to the faculty of true awareness found in people. “True awareness” means the feeling or consciousness present in the body, which functions using blood, bone, nerves, muscle and skin as instruments.

This consciousness differs somewhat and in large measure between human beings and other animals because their bodily structures differ. The body's organs produce awareness, and each body receives it according to their arrangement. Since the human body is formed with the capacity to use the awareness it obtains in an appropriate manner, humans possess true awareness.

One may ask why this human awareness is not simply called *meyyunarvu*, true awareness, but *kadavul*. The essay analyzes *kadavul* as a compound of *kada* and *ul*: something that stands beyond the mind. Since awareness alone stands beyond and surpasses the mind, it received this name.

An awareness absent from a person at one time may arise at another. Thus awareness can stand beyond the person's present mind and later enter it. The graceful word *kadavul*, the writer argues, expresses without distortion this real thing that stands beyond the mind.

This point requires some explanation. We see that awareness absent from a person at one time is present later. Here awareness means knowledge. The difference may partly depend upon the intellectual state of observer and observed, but that is not a complete cause.

A person is born in ignorance. Knowledge gradually arises, or the person is encouraged to reach the fields that create it. Thereafter comes rational intelligence, which understands what has been learned. Reason stands as knowledge of knowledge and guides the person along a good path.

Thus awareness initially beyond the person's mind comes to reside within through effort, which serves as a balance. No action yields benefit beyond the effort applied to it; knowledge too comes to each person in proportion to effort. Hence people stand at differing intellectual levels.

The causes of differences in knowledge are individual effort and bodily constitution, not the religious notions of karma and fate. Everyone who makes an effort can shine as a jewel of humanity according to that effort. Textual claims that teach foolishness will therefore be rejected by intelligent people. The meaning religious writers assign to *kadavul* is not only imaginary but deceptive.

Tiruvalluvar, the essay continues, provides support for the claim that a person is born without knowledge, acquires it later, initially finds it beyond the mind, and can attain it. It cites the couplet whose key line is “To perceive the true substance is knowledge.”

Its meaning is that knowledge consists in leaving the ignorance arising at birth, using reason to perceive the proper truth that gives excellence, and conducting oneself accordingly.

People unable to grasp this truth interpret it according to their doctrines, confuse themselves and harm others. The couplet adorns Valluvar's chapter on true awareness. Only shallow minds transfer it into the sphere of religion, which teaches ignorance.

Saivites use the phrase *semporul* in the couplet, insert their doctrine into it and bury the people's true awareness. They apply the term to the Siva they worship and declare him the “perfect substance,” trapping people in a religious net.

What greater murder of Tamil could there be? What connection can exist between *semporul*, which refers to human true awareness, and this Siva described as red or fire-shaped, distinct from humanity and not the Siva said to have been a Sangam poet?

*Semporul* means a proper or excellent substance. Since nothing in the world is more excellent than knowledge, the distinguished word belongs to true awareness.

If, as Saivite writers claim, *semporul* denotes Siva worshipped as God, why did Tiruvalluvar, who used the word, say nothing about Saivism, Siva, other religions or their gods?

The term does not occur in the present chapter called “Praise of God” in the *Tirukkural*. It occurs in the chapter “True Awareness,” which concerns the reason people need. If it were a fitting name for the Saivite God and Valluvar shared that meaning, why would it appear under true awareness rather than praise of God?

Nor would someone who composed a praise of God and taught people to follow it need to compose separately upon true awareness. A person who acts by true awareness has no need of divine aid, but performs work with personal intelligence.

The essay further reports a scholarly conclusion that “Praise of God” and other chapters expressing religious and unnatural concepts were interpolations not composed by Tiruvalluvar, though a full discussion would be too extensive here.

It also asks readers to note that the *Tolkappiyam*, the grammatical counterpart to the literary *Tirukkural*, contains no protective invocation resembling “Praise of God.” If the god described by religious writers had existed in Tamil Nadu in Tolkappiyar's time, would he not have placed at least one hymn of invocation before his unequalled work explaining Tamil structure and the people's noble qualities?

The *Tolkappiyam* makes clear that ancient Tamils did not live by claiming nonexistent things existed and deceiving people for gain. The essay therefore concludes that no doctrine of God existed in Tamil Nadu in Tolkappiyar's age, and that present worship and doctrine were later brought by Aryans.

By Tiruvalluvar's period, such doctrine had spread to some extent, allowing references to be interpolated into the *Tirukkural*. The structure of the older, distinctively Tamil *Tolkappiyam*, created when ancient Tamils held no such doctrine, prevented medieval scholars from inserting it there.

The writer therefore maintains that *kadavul* is an excellent Tamil word expressing human true awareness through the combined assistance of body and mind.

He gives a similar analysis of *andavan*. Just as *kadavul* is treated as a combination of *kada* and *ul*, *andavan* is divided into *andu* and *avan*. The former is interpreted as there, beyond or beyond the mind, and the latter as knowledge. Thus the word is said to signify knowledge existing beyond a person's mind and later arriving through effort.

Why, then, did religious writers apply *kadavul*, a word said to refer only to human true awareness, to their deity?

They needed to construct religions upon the doctrine that a thing beyond human knowledge exists somewhere under the name God and directs humanity. Seeking an appropriate Tamil word for this imagined entity, cunning Aryans and some Tamils who followed them reasoned: “Knowledge stands beyond the mind and is called *kadavul* or *andavan*. We too proclaim that a powerful great entity stands beyond living minds and directs the world. Since the Tamil word for true awareness partly suits our doctrine, let us employ it.”

They used these words easily and made their doctrine succeed. Many Tamil words containing excellent meanings thus fell into religious hands and lost their true usage.

For example, the Tamil word *iraivan* referred to a king. After religious doctrine arose here, it came chiefly to denote the imaginary god of religious writers. Tiruvalluvar placed the word in the political chapter “The Greatness of the King.” Yet when people utter it now, it immediately attaches itself to the invented deity.

Likewise, the essay says, *kadavul*, denoting true awareness, and *andavan*, denoting knowledge and ruler, have lost their original meanings.

Until Tamil people receive education and the true awareness produced by it, deeply rooted doubts of this kind cannot easily be removed by any means other than legislation. Considering these matters confirms the necessity of national separation. Once our country becomes our own, such doubts can easily be removed and many benefits given to our people.

Religious texts say, inconsistently, “If sought, he will be found.” People who believed and followed that path could not find what they sought, grew weary and described their disappointment. Pattinathar, Tirunavukkarasar, Buddha and Tayumanavar are named among those who travelled and returned from that path.

Peeling the sheaths of a fruitless banana tree one by one reveals nothing within except the removed layers. In the same way, those who followed the road prescribed by religion stated clearly and thoughtfully that the God they expected was not found.

From the day the claim arose that a nonexistent thing existed, it has been denied. Yet religious doctrine has split the relationship between truth and the world, allowing the foolishness of accepting falsehood as truth to gain prestige.

Establishing truths therefore requires great effort, labour and courage, and a willingness to accept hardship. The modern world visibly gains some success because rational thought, long decaying among the people, has been made an ornament of inquiry and uses its rays to overthrow the world's fallen doctrines.

The writer concludes, trusting that the preceding discussion explains the word *kadavul*, what it signifies, and the strategy through which religious writers used it for their own object. He therefore does not elaborate further.

5 November 1944.

---NOTES---
The scan directly resolves a missing OCR clause on the final page as `“தேடினால் கிடைப்பர்”`; this was restored in both canonical Tamil copies before generating the bilingual file.

The analyses of `கடவுள்` as `கட + உள்`, `ஆண்டவன்` as `ஆண்டு + அவன்`, and `இறைவன்` as originally “king” are translated as the essay's etymological claims. They are not presented as conclusions of modern historical linguistics.

The OCR rendering of the cited *Tirukkural* is damaged and includes stray numerals. The English translates the key phrase and the interpretation supplied by the article without silently reconstructing an exact critical-edition couplet in the Tamil source.
